course: dictionary
lesson: 8726
title: Sepsis: when the fight goes wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Your body has a defence system. When germs get in through a cut or an infection, your body sends fighters to kill them. Sepsis is a very serious illness that happens when that fight goes wrong and starts to harm the body's own organs.
explain: Think of a smoke alarm that will not stop ringing and starts damaging the house. The alarm is meant to protect you, but here it causes harm. That is why sepsis is an emergency: it can get worse very quickly.
explain: Read the clues in this sentence: «A rash that doesn't fade under a pressed glass, with fever, can mean meningitis or sepsis, very dangerous infections, and needs 999 or A&E straight away.» The words very dangerous and straight away tell you that sepsis is serious and that you must get help at once.
fact: Sepsis is a noun, and it is usually uncountable: you say sepsis, not a sepsis.
fact: «Sepsis» means a very serious illness that happens when the body's fight against infection goes wrong and harms its own organs.
fact: Older books use sepsis to mean any poisoning of the body by germs from a wound or infection.
fact: Health posters say «Think sepsis» and list warning signs: very fast breathing, mottled or bluish skin, confusion, and feeling worse than ever before.
fact: Use the emergency number your advice gives, such as 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA or 000 in Australia.
exercise: «A rash that doesn't fade under a pressed glass, with fever, can mean meningitis or sepsis.» What is sepsis? | A type of sweet drink | A kind of sunburn | *A very serious illness when the body's fight against infection goes wrong | A cheap plaster for small cuts
exercise: Health posters say «Think sepsis». Which is a warning sign? | Playing outside in the sun | Laughing at a funny joke | *Confusion and feeling worse than ever before | Wanting a snack before lunch
exercise: Why should cuts be kept clean? | So they look nicer in photos | *To help stop germs getting in and causing infection | So the skin changes colour on purpose
test: In an old story, a character dies from a small wound. What might the author mean? | The character was struck by lightning | The character slept too long | *Germs from the wound poisoned the body | The character ate too many sweets
test: The sentence says sepsis «needs 999 or A&E straight away». What should you do? | Wait and see if it goes away by itself | *Get help at once by calling 999 or going to A&E | Go to bed and sleep it off | Wash the rash with cold tea
exam: Which statement about sepsis is true? | *Sepsis is an emergency, so get help quickly | Sepsis is harmless if you ignore it | Sepsis is a kind of sweet cake | Sepsis is a happy word for a sunny day
